---

Zazz installs three ways. All produce the same kit; they differ in who owns the files.

| | Files live | Updates | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **CLI** | in your repo (`zazz/`) | three-way merge with the published version | apps you will customize and keep |
| **CDN** | on jsDelivr, pinned | change a version string | prototypes, docs, CMS pages |
| **npm** | in `node_modules` | `pnpm update` | bundled apps that treat the kit as a dependency |

## CLI

The `zazz-ui` CLI copies primitives from `@zazz-ui/core` into your project so you own the code, and records where every file came from so later updates merge instead of overwrite.

```bash
pnpm dlx zazz-ui init
pnpm dlx zazz-ui add button card dialog
```

`init` vendors the base platform into `zazz/`: the layers, tokens, reset, typography, the generated utilities, the core runtime scripts, an `index.css` and `index.js` pair, and a `head.html` snippet to paste into your `<head>`. It also writes `zazz.json`, the kit version plus a hash of every vendored file's original bytes. Flags: `--dir <path>`, `--ts` (TypeScript sources instead of emitted JavaScript, plus the kit's ambient `globals.d.ts`), `--legacy <path>` (wire an existing stylesheet into the `legacy.imports` sublayer), `--no-fonts` (omit the Geist block from `head.html`), `--no-theme-script`.

`add <primitive…>` resolves dependencies (each primitive's API page lists what its command brings; `combobox` pulls in six), copies the files at your recorded version, and inserts the imports at the right cascade position. Each primitive comes with its data file, `primitives/<name>/<name>.json`, which lists its hooks with their defaults, its attributes and their values, its slots, and its states. `--examples` also copies the example fragments.

`update [@version] [names…]` moves the project, or the named primitives, to a new version. Because `zazz.json` knows which files you edited, untouched files take the new version silently, your edits survive where upstream did not move, and non-overlapping changes merge. On a real conflict the CLI asks you to choose: write conflict markers, keep yours, take theirs, or skip (`--markers`, `--keep`, `--theirs` answer for non-interactive runs). The relevant slice of the changelog prints first, breaking changes flagged. `diff` shows the same read-only.

`codemod data-font` moves a project's markup to typography roles in `data-font`: `data-ui="text-sm"` becomes `data-font="sm"`. It updates the kit first if the vendored version predates `data-font`, then vendors `font`, plus `link` and `prose` if the markup uses them. It lists anything it can't rewrite safely, such as two roles on one element or a role set from script, leaves it unchanged, and exits with code 2. `--dry-run` lists the files it would change.

`codemod small-sizes` deals with the small control sizes the kit removed (`data-button-size` and `data-toggle-size` `"sm"` and `"icon-sm"`). It asks what to do, and removing them is the default: `"sm"` is dropped and `"icon-sm"` becomes `"icon"` in the markup. To keep them instead, pick that at the prompt or pass `--restore`. In a vendored project the removed rules go back into the vendored `button.css` and `toggle.css`. Without a `zazz.json` they go in `zazz-small-sizes.css`, which you load after the kit's stylesheet. Fields never had a small size, so they are left alone. A selector or a size set from script is listed for you to change by hand, with exit code 2.

There is no runtime dependency on the package. Edit the files, delete what you do not need, commit them.

## CDN

Two requests load the whole kit from jsDelivr: the single-file stylesheet and script. This is the `<head>` the kit's `buildHead()` produces for the installed version:

{% head mode="cdn" /%}

To load only the primitives a page uses, name them. The kit's manifest resolves each primitive's dependencies, and every URL is pinned and carries an integrity hash:

{% head mode="cdn" primitives="button card dialog" /%}

{% callout type="warning" %}
Always pin an exact version. The import map's integrity hashes are per byte, so `@latest` would break on the next release; unpinned URLs also defeat jsDelivr's permanent caching.
{% /callout %}

## npm

```bash
pnpm add @zazz-ui/core
```

```js
import "@zazz-ui/core/index.css";
import "@zazz-ui/core"; // the behavior
```

The package also exports the single-file bundles (`@zazz-ui/core/zazz.css`, `@zazz-ui/core/zazz.js`), individual files (`@zazz-ui/core/primitives/dialog/dialog.css`, `@zazz-ui/core/base/signals.js`), the editor data (`@zazz-ui/core/editor/*`), and the head builder (`@zazz-ui/core/head`). Bundlers resolve the kit's one bare dependency (`signal-polyfill`) from `node_modules`, so no import map is needed.

{% callout %}
Keep the kit's stylesheet out of CSS minifiers that rewrite modern syntax. `@property` registrations, `light-dark()`, and relative `oklch(from …)` colors are load-bearing; the published `dist/zazz.css` is already minified with them intact.
{% /callout %}

## The head contract

Whichever route you take, a Zazz page's `<head>` has the same parts, in this order: the meta tags, any font links, the stylesheet link(s), the import map, a `modulepreload` hint, the interest-invokers polyfill, the module script, and the theme script. `buildHead()` from `@zazz-ui/core/head` writes it; the CLI's `head.html` is that output; this site's own head is too. The theme script must stay inline and last so `data-theme` lands before first paint. Under a strict Content Security Policy, the two inline scripts take a nonce (`buildHead({ nonce })`) and the `style` attribute needs `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'`; the [security page](/docs/install/security/) has the policy and what the kit does without it.

For a vendored copy at `./zazz/`:

{% head /%}
